=== Lease Contracts ===

Titles can be leased out by the holder to another character. (Only non-capital baronies, and the holder must be above baron rank.)
Income and levies from the leased holdings can be split between the ruler, lessee,
and (for hierarchical leases) a "lease liege" or "top lease liege"

== Lease Hierarchies ==

Built-in lease types can be used to automatically lease out holdings according to a specific hierarchy.
In that case there is a lease vassal hierarchy in parallel to the normal vassal hierarchy.
There are two supported Lease Hierarchies types, one that follows the regular title vassal structure, and one that
follows the clerical region structure.

= Vassal based Lease Hierarchy =
--------|-------------------------------------------------------------
Rulers  | Lease Hierarchy
--------|-------------------------------------------------------------
        | Head of Faith (if they exist)
        |  ↑
Emperor ─ Emperor's lessee
↑       |  ↑
King ──── King's lessee
↑       |  ↑
Duke ──── Duke's lessee
↑       |  ↑
Count ─── Count's lessee
↑       |
Baron ─── (there is no 'Baron' level Lessee, all are leased by Count+ level's lessees)

- Taxes/Levies flow up through characters.
- Rulers in the Vassal hierarchy can be skipped using another trigger.
  E.g. for the theocratic lease the ruler and vassal must have the same faith.
  The skipped rulers will be part of their own hierarchy with matching rulers in the same realm.
- In this hierarchy the lessee must be a direct vassal of the ruler.
- If the lessee is missing for a specific ruler the ruler is also skipped in the hierarchy.
- Rulers can be excluded from participating in the automatic lease with a trigger, e.g. only rulers with a specific
  faith property might take part in a theocratic lease.
- Intermediate lessees with a different faith are skipped - if the Count's lessee is faith A, the Duke's is faith B
  and the King's is faith A again, the Count's lessee's superior is the King's lessee

= Clerical Region based Lease Hierarchy =
--------|-------------------------------------------------------------
Rulers  | Lease Hierarchy
--------|-------------------------------------------------------------
        | Head of Faith (if they exist)
        |  ↑
        | Clerical Region Title holder (if they exist)
        |                   ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑
Emperor ─ Emperor's lessee ─┘ │ │ │
        |                     │ │ │
King ──── King's lessee ──────┘ │ │
        |                       │ │
Duke ──── Duke's lessee ────────┘ │
        |                         │
Count ─── Count's lessee ─────────┘
        |
Baron ──── (there is no 'Baron' level Lessee, all are leased by Count+ level's lessees)

- Instead of flowing through the vassal' structure, taxes & levies flow from lessees to the Clerical Region title holder.
- The Clerical Region Title holder is determined by lessee's ruler's capital.
- If there is no Clerical Region title holder, it flows onwards to the Head of Faith, if they exist.
- Rulers can be excluded from participating in the automatic lease with a trigger, e.g. only rulers with a specific
  faith property might take part in a theocratic lease.

== Structure ==

theocracy_lease = {
	# Definition for automatic lease hierarchy. Required for some built-in lease contracts, not supported for the others.
	hierarchy = {
		# What type of hierarchy is followed. (see above)
		# Default: vassal
		type = vassal/clerical_region

		# Does this ruler use the lease? root: character; default always
		ruler_valid = { <triggers> } = yes

		# The character leasing the titles from the ruler. root: character. Must be unique for a given ruler, and their liege must be the ruler
		lessee = <event target>

		# Is this ruler valid as a liege or vassal to the scope:target? If not, this ruler is skipped in the lease hierarchy and starts their own hierarchy.
		# root and scope:target are characters; default always = yes
		liege_or_vassal_valid = { <triggers> }

		# Is this barony title automatically leased out? root: title default always = yes
		barony_valid = { <triggers> }
	}

	# Government type associated with this lease. If it has the theocracy mechanic, the valid_holdings below
	# are treated as theocratic lease holding types.
	government = theocracy_government

	# List of holding types that will not generate a wrong holding type penalty for the lessee
	valid_holdings = { church_holding }

	# Used in the UI to show why the ruler receives benefits from the leased titles. Make sure it corresponds to the values used below.
	# Don't set if you don't use the same opinion value between levies and taxes or the conditions are different, so that the tooltip is not shown.
	ruler_share_min_opinion_from_lessee = 42

	# Used in the UI to show if a ruler should receive the benefits by having a hook even if they do not meet the opinion value. Make sure it gets used to apply based on the values below.
	hook_strength_max_opinion = none/any/strong

	# How income from leased holdings is split. By default everything goes to the lessee.
	#
	# The three shares below are one flat split of the whole value; the Lessee keeps whatever they do
	# not claim. A share that evaluates below 0 is treated as 0. If the three together exceed 1.0 a
	# content error is logged and they are truncated in the order lease_liege, top_lease_liege_direct,
	# ruler until they fit.
	#
	# Each share takes an optional `<share>_max` ceiling (default: no ceiling). Put the ceiling there
	# rather than as a `min`/`max` inside the share's own block: those are operations applied at their
	# position in the sequence rather than bounds on the result, and they cannot be read back out for
	# the 'up to' tooltips.
	#
	# Any of the dynamic weight-calculations below can put a `desc = LOC_KEY` inside an individual
	# operand block (`add = { desc = MY_KEY  value = 0.1 }`) to have that term named in the split
	# tooltip. Operands without a `desc` are not shown at all.
	tax_split = {
		# Share that goes to the lease liege (if existing). Requires hierarchy definition. Default: 0
		# Top lease liege (head of faith) is excluded from this (use `top_lease_liege` or `top_lease_liege_direct` instead)
		lease_liege = 0.0-1.0
		# Or use dynamic weight-calculation
		# Scopes:
		# - lessee - character leasing or religious subordinate
		# - lease_liege - character higher in the lease hierarchy (religious superior)
		lease_liege = { ... }
		lease_liege_max = 0.0-1.0

		# Share that goes directly to the top lease liege (if existing). Requires hierarchy definition. Default: 0
		# Will add to 'lease_liege' for the lessees under the top lease liege
		top_lease_liege_direct = 0.0-1.0
		# Or use dynamic weight-calculation
		# Scopes:
		# - lessee - character leasing or religious subordinate
		# - lease_liege - highest single higher in the lease hierarchy (head of faith). Note: scoped as lease_liege, not top_lease_liege
		top_lease_liege_direct = { ... }
		top_lease_liege_direct_max = 0.0-1.0

		# Share that goes to the ruler leasing the holding out.
		# Scopes:
		# - lessee - character leasing
		# - ruler - character leasing out the holding
		ruler = { ... }
		# This one doubles as the figure the 'up to' tooltips quote.
		ruler_max = 0.0-1.0
	}

	# How levies from leased holdings are assigned. By default everything goes to the lessee.
	levy_split = { ... }	# See tax_split for syntax
	# Note: Levies can only go to one character on each level. This means levies cannot be assigned in part to the ruler and in part to the lessee
	# because they are considered to be on the same level. So you can use only 0 or 100 when splitting between ruler and lessee.
}
